title: Proposition 8
---
order: 8
---
short_label: X.8
---
sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: If two magnitudes do not have to one another the ratio which a number has to a number, then the magnitudes are incommensurable.
----
proof:

Let the two magnitudes *A* and *B* not have to one another the ratio which a number has to a number.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="160" height="100" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="X.8 diagram" src="propX7.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "X.8",
        elements: [
            "A0;point;free;-1000,40;0;0",
            "A3;point;free;1000,40;0;0",
            "B0;point;free;-1000,70;0;0",
            "B3;point;free;1000,70;0;0",
            "A1;point;lineSlider;A0,A3,30,0;0",
            "A2;point;lineSlider;A0,A3,130,0;0",
            "A;line;connect;A1,A2;black",
            "B1;point;lineSlider;B0,B3,30,0;0",
            "B2;point;lineSlider;B0,B3,109,0;0",
            "B;line;connect;B1,B2;black"
        ],
    });
</script>
</figure>

I say that the magnitudes *A* and *B* are incommensurable.

For, if they are commensurable, then *A* has to *B* the ratio which a number has to a number. [!just X.5]

But it does not, therefore the magnitudes *A* and *B* are incommensurable.

Therefore, *if two magnitudes do not have to one another the ratio which a number has to a number, then the magnitudes are incommensurable.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
----
guide: This proposition is the contrapositive of @X.5. It is used in frequently in @X.11.
